## Proof

We determine the values of $p \in [1,+\infty[$ for which $f(x)=|x|$ belongs to $W^{1,p}(]-1,1[)$.

**Step 1: $f \in L^p(]-1,1[)$ for all $p \in [1,+\infty[$.**

The function $f(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$, hence bounded ($|f(x)|\le 1$). Since $]-1,1[$ is bounded, $f\in L^p(]-1,1[)$ for every $p\in[1,+\infty[$.

**Step 2: Compute the distributional derivative $Df$.**

By definition, for every test function $\varphi\in\mathcal{D}(]-1,1[)$:
$$\langle Df,\varphi\rangle = -\int_{-1}^{1}|x|\,\varphi'(x)\,dx = -\int_{-1}^{0}(-x)\varphi'(x)\,dx - \int_{0}^{1}x\,\varphi'(x)\,dx.$$

**First integral.** Integrating by parts on $[-1,0]$:
$$\int_{-1}^{0}x\,\varphi'(x)\,dx = \bigl[x\varphi(x)\bigr]_{-1}^{0}-\int_{-1}^{0}\varphi(x)\,dx = 0\cdot\varphi(0)-(-1)\varphi(-1)-\int_{-1}^{0}\varphi(x)\,dx.$$

Since $\varphi$ has compact support in $]-1,1[$, $\varphi(-1)=0$, so:
$$-\int_{-1}^{0}(-x)\varphi'(x)\,dx = -\int_{-1}^{0}\varphi(x)\,dx.$$

**Second integral.** Integrating by parts on $[0,1]$:
$$-\int_{0}^{1}x\,\varphi'(x)\,dx = -\bigl[x\varphi(x)\bigr]_{0}^{1}+\int_{0}^{1}\varphi(x)\,dx = -\varphi(1)+0\cdot\varphi(0)+\int_{0}^{1}\varphi(x)\,dx.$$

Since $\varphi(1)=0$:
$$-\int_{0}^{1}x\,\varphi'(x)\,dx = \int_{0}^{1}\varphi(x)\,dx.$$

**Combining:**
$$\langle Df,\varphi\rangle = -\int_{-1}^{0}\varphi(x)\,dx+\int_{0}^{1}\varphi(x)\,dx = \int_{-1}^{1}\operatorname{sgn}(x)\,\varphi(x)\,dx,$$

where $\operatorname{sgn}(x)=-1$ for $x<0$ and $\operatorname{sgn}(x)=1$ for $x>0$.

**Key observation:** There is **no Dirac delta term** at $x=0$ because $f(x)=|x|$ is **continuous** at the origin. (A delta term would arise only from a jump discontinuity.) Thus:
$$Df = \operatorname{sgn}(x) \quad \text{in } \mathcal{D}'(]-1,1[).$$

**Step 3: $\operatorname{sgn}\in L^p(]-1,1[)$ for all $p\in[1,+\infty[$.**

The function $\operatorname{sgn}(x)$ is bounded ($|\operatorname{sgn}(x)|=1$ a.e.) and $]-1,1[$ has finite measure, so:
$$\int_{-1}^{1}|\operatorname{sgn}(x)|^p\,dx = \int_{-1}^{1}1\,dx = 2 < +\infty \quad \text{for all } p\in[1,+\infty[.$$

**Conclusion.**

Both $f$ and its distributional derivative $Df=\operatorname{sgn}$ belong to $L^p(]-1,1[)$ for every $p\in[1,+\infty[$. Therefore:

$$\boxed{f(x)=|x|\in W^{1,p}(]-1,1[) \text{ for all } p\in[1,+\infty[.}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
